**定理 83.1（uniform coarse-error alphabet）。** For either plateau, ALL source sizes, let E=1/2+tau and 0<tau<=1/8 (larger E can use tau=1/8). A finite full-word alphabet of O(log(1/tau)) bits suffices under source-aware selection or the separately paid fine-name archive, without a contraction gap. Its finite-alphabet minimum is Theta(log(1/tau)) as tau->0. This law has its own finite-alphabet proof.

Explicit net: every orthogonal future phase basis is a rotation times an optional reflection of the third column. Represent an SO(3) rotation by a unit quaternion q. Choose Q=ceil(32/tau) and integers n_i by rounding Qq_i, |n_i|<=Q+1. The normalized n/Q differs from q by <=2/Q. The quaternion rotation map is quadratic with operator Lipschitz bound 4 on unit quaternions (expand its action qvq^-1), so its basis differs by <=8/Q<=tau/4. The finite selection procedure computes the rational rotation directly by the integer quadratic formula divided by sum n_i^2, with no normalization or square root. Enumerating all nonzero integer quadruples in [-Q-1,Q+1]^4 and both reflections gives a finite explicit net. If an actual anchor is zero, complete its phase directions to any orthogonal basis; no choice is needed by the encoder to construct such a completion.

Use centers c_r=half the rational unit basis columns. Every actual future of phase r is on [0,A_r], |A_r|<=1. With the net orientation both endpoint distances are <=1/2+tau/8. The finite selection procedure chooses p so 2^-p<=tau/16 and requests b=2p+24. The sign/anchor estimates above give approximate-anchor error <tau/16. It enumerates the explicit rational net and accepts the first pair whose three squared distances to those approximate anchors are <=(1/2+tau/2)^2. A valid pair necessarily exists. Every accepted pair is within 1/2+9tau/16 of the true heads, and has distance exactly 1/2 from zero. Convexity bounds the entire phase segment; output rounding adds <tau/16. Hence the entire future error is <E. Tie acceptance is <=, the candidate order is the explicit order in §76, requests and returned bits are O(log(1/tau)), and no true orientation or equality oracle is an input. Its intentionally crude finite enumeration time is O(tau^-4 poly(log(1/tau))); installation and query workspace are polynomial in log(1/tau), and output width O(log(1/tau)). The complete orientation word records four signed integers, reflection, output precision and mode. No hidden runtime table is used.

For the lower, color each direction by a recurrent word on its actual contracting sequence, as in the finite-alphabet proof. Any two directions with that color must have an enclosing ball with 0 of radius <=E. When E<sqrt(2)/2, the obtuse branch is impossible; the acute formula gives $\sin^2(\theta/2)\leq1-1/(4E^2)=O(\tau)$. Thus each color's directions have chordal diameter O(sqrt(tau)) and lie in a spherical cap of area O(tau). Covering the sphere requires at least c/tau colors, so B>=log2(1/tau)-O(1). This proof uses finitely many words; it does not prohibit individually finite source-specific words at smaller tolerances. The orientation representation is nonredundant for uniform E>1/2 because it avoids gap acquisition and scale-dependent work; it does not improve the small-E matched law.

